    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW28E8spDYf7rHb581FEggEr7)_\n\n## Merge order\n\n- No dependency on other open PRs; touches `sp-async-work` and `sp-batch-report` only.\n\n## How the batch commands run\n\n- There is no `sp-batch-process`. The engine is `sp-async-work` (in-process timers); `sp-batch-report` is only the report buffer.\n- `sp-author-notes` and the other litnote linters run as **timer slices inside the same Emacs** (50 ms per slice), not as a subprocess. That is why nothing shows in `proced`.\n- Only the footnote lint starts a second Emacs (`make-process`).\n\n## Why C-g did not work\n\n- Emacs runs timer functions with `inhibit-quit` = `t` (checked on Emacs 29.3).\n- A `C-g` during a slice only set a flag, acted on after the slice. Between slices it is a plain keypress.\n- A quit also skipped the rescheduling, so the job stayed \"running\" with no timer (a zombie that blocked a rerun).\n\n## What changed\n\n- A slice runs with `inhibit-quit` nil; a `C-g` in a slice stops the job cleanly.\n- Two `C-g` in a row (outside the minibuffer) stop all jobs (`sp-async-work-quit-cancels`).\n- `M-x sp-batch-cancel` (alias of `sp-async-work-cancel`) and `k` in the report buffer (header line and echo-area hints).\n- Running jobs write `var/batch-jobs/ID.job` (key=value lines, at most once a second, removed at the end); stale files of dead Emacs processes are cleaned on listing.\n- `M-x sp-batch-list-jobs`: jobs of all Emacs instances, `k` stops one (a cancel file for another Emacs, direct for this one).\n- `tools/sp-batch-jobs [list | cancel ID|--all]`: plain `sh`, works from fish.\n- Options considered (cancel file, `emacsclient`, `kill -USR2`, `kill`) are bullets in `docs/tangles/funct/sp-async-work.org`.\n\n## Not tested\n\n- Real `C-g` from a GUI Emacs on a real vault run (only simulated: `(signal 'quit)` inside a slice, and `last-command`).\n- A job stuck inside one huge item cannot be stopped from outside; it only checks between items.\n- The echo-area hints in the report buffer and the `*Batch jobs*` buffer were not looked at in a GUI.\n- macOS `process-attributes` for the stale-PID check.\n\n## Tests run\n\n- New `tests/sp-async-work-registry-test.el` (7), `tests/sp-async-work-test.el` (10) and `tests/sp-batch-report-test.el` (17) pass on Emacs 29.3.\n- The script was run against a real `emacs --batch` job: list, cancel by name part, job ended as cancelled.\n\n## Manual testing\n\n- [ ] Start `M-x sp-author-notes`, press `C-g` while it reads: the job stops with \"cancelled (C-g)\".\n- [ ] Rerun it straight after: it starts (no \"still running\").\n- [ ] Press `C-g` once while idle: nothing stops; twice in a row: the job stops.\n- [ ] In the report buffer press `k` while a job runs: it stops; the echo area lists `k Stop running job`.\n- [ ] Start a job, open a second Emacs, `M-x sp-batch-list-jobs`, press `k` on it: the first Emacs stops it within a second.\n- [ ] In fish: `tools/sp-batch-jobs`, then `tools/sp-batch-jobs cancel author`.\n- [ ] Kill the Emacs of a job with `kill -9`: the next `sp-batch-jobs` drops the stale entry.\n- [ ] `ls var/batch-jobs` is empty when no job runs.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiBhjMEvTpuoTULaVya)_",
